WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.311 --> 00:00:03.439
- He's a Computer Scientist
and Internet Entrepreneur.

00:00:03.439 --> 00:00:06.349
He's ranked 19th on
Forbes Billionaires List,

00:00:06.349 --> 00:00:10.561
with an estimated net worth
of 33.4 billion dollars.

00:00:10.561 --> 00:00:13.429
He leads a company with over 57,000

00:00:13.429 --> 00:00:16.165
employees in over 40 countries.

00:00:16.165 --> 00:00:18.285
He's Google co-founder Larry Page,

00:00:18.285 --> 00:00:21.785
and here are his Top 10 Rules for Success.

00:00:24.392 --> 00:00:26.118
- A lot of times, my
issue is people are not

00:00:26.118 --> 00:00:29.785
setting goals that will
be something that...

00:00:30.994 --> 00:00:35.394
Somebody just becomes
rich, or could do anything.

00:00:35.394 --> 00:00:38.761
You got to be excited about
working on that for 10 years.

00:00:38.761 --> 00:00:41.320
So you got to have a big enough goal.

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:43.065
That, you know, you
attract the best people

00:00:43.065 --> 00:00:46.220
and you retain them, and
you keep them focused.

00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:48.078
In my experience, you
don't go wrong, you know...

00:00:48.078 --> 00:00:51.744
Maybe you don't hit it
next year, or maybe you

00:00:51.744 --> 00:00:53.825
fail entirely and you discover something

00:00:53.825 --> 00:00:57.296
more exciting to work on
with the same set of people.

00:00:57.296 --> 00:00:59.504
One of the the things
that I'd been through

00:00:59.504 --> 00:01:01.662
as a student was some leadership training,

00:01:01.662 --> 00:01:04.140
and one of the things they
taught us was to have...

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:07.791
To not be afraid of failure,
and instead have the goal

00:01:07.791 --> 00:01:11.705
to fail a lot quickly, and
then eventually you'll succeed.

00:01:11.705 --> 00:01:13.852
And I sort of took this to
heart, and they also had

00:01:13.852 --> 00:01:17.463
a slogan called "Healthy
Disregard for the Impossible."

00:01:17.463 --> 00:01:19.721
They actually made you write
down, sort of, the things

00:01:19.721 --> 00:01:21.573
you would do that were kind of impossible

00:01:21.573 --> 00:01:24.073
but you thought you
might really accomplish.

00:01:24.073 --> 00:01:26.872
And that's really stuck
with me in everything

00:01:26.872 --> 00:01:30.536
that I've tried to do,
and I think, you know...

00:01:30.536 --> 00:01:32.137
It was very, very close
that we wouldn't have

00:01:32.137 --> 00:01:35.354
started the company, and I
think there are many of you

00:01:35.354 --> 00:01:37.705
out there in sort of similar situations.

00:01:37.705 --> 00:01:40.271
You know, do you want to
take a little bit more risk?

00:01:40.271 --> 00:01:42.570
Do you want to try something else?

00:01:42.570 --> 00:01:44.195
And, you know, even if
you don't succeed...

00:01:44.195 --> 00:01:47.345
We actually many things that didn't work.

00:01:47.345 --> 00:01:49.831
Google happened to work pretty well,

00:01:49.831 --> 00:01:52.053
but there are many things
we did that didn't.

00:01:52.053 --> 00:01:54.237
But we don't worry about those, right?

00:01:54.237 --> 00:01:55.751
'Cause we tried many things.

00:01:55.751 --> 00:01:57.552
So I just encourage you to take a little

00:01:57.552 --> 00:02:00.460
more risk in life, and I think if you

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:03.389
do it often enough, it
will really pay off.

00:02:03.389 --> 00:02:05.900
This is how we keep
our innovation running.

00:02:05.900 --> 00:02:08.198
I think, usually, as companies get bigger,

00:02:08.198 --> 00:02:12.285
they find it really hard to
have small innovative projects.

00:02:12.285 --> 00:02:14.466
We had this problem, too,
for a while, and we said,

00:02:14.466 --> 00:02:16.776
"Oh, we really need a new concept."

00:02:16.776 --> 00:02:19.425
The Google Ads, that's a
small project that we're not

00:02:19.425 --> 00:02:22.647
quite sure if it's going to work
or not, but we hope it will.

00:02:22.647 --> 00:02:25.114
And if we do enough of
them, some of them will

00:02:25.114 --> 00:02:27.645
really work and turn out such as news.

00:02:27.645 --> 00:02:29.879
But then we had a problem
because then we had

00:02:29.879 --> 00:02:34.038
over 100 projects, and I
don't know about all of you,

00:02:34.038 --> 00:02:37.298
but I have trouble keeping
100 things in my head at once.

00:02:37.298 --> 00:02:40.084
And we found that if we
just wrote all of them down,

00:02:40.084 --> 00:02:43.120
and ordered them, and
these are kind of made up.

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:45.557
Don't really pay attention to them.

00:02:45.557 --> 00:02:49.217
For example, the "Buy Iceland"
was from a media article.

00:02:49.217 --> 00:02:51.278
We would never do such
a crazy thing, but...

00:02:51.278 --> 00:02:53.303
(audience laughs)

00:02:53.303 --> 00:02:54.581
(inaudible)

00:02:54.581 --> 00:02:56.915
We found if we just
basically wrote them all down

00:02:56.915 --> 00:02:59.413
and ordered them, that most people would

00:02:59.413 --> 00:03:01.849
actually agree what
the ordering should be.

00:03:01.849 --> 00:03:04.367
And this was kind of a
surprise to me, but we found

00:03:04.367 --> 00:03:06.812
that as long as you keep
the 100 things in your head,

00:03:06.812 --> 00:03:08.670
which you did by writing them down,

00:03:08.670 --> 00:03:10.475
that you could do a
pretty good job deciding

00:03:10.475 --> 00:03:13.383
what to do and where
to put your resources.

00:03:13.383 --> 00:03:15.290
And, so that's basically what we've done,

00:03:15.290 --> 00:03:17.433
Since (inaudible) did
that a few years ago,

00:03:17.433 --> 00:03:19.668
and I think it's really
allowed us to be innovative

00:03:19.668 --> 00:03:22.284
and still stay reasonable well-organized.

00:03:22.284 --> 00:03:25.980
You know I talked a little bit
about focusing the company.

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:29.030
And you know, I think that I want to talk

00:03:29.030 --> 00:03:32.538
a little bit about making some big bets.

00:03:32.538 --> 00:03:35.189
We always try to concentrate
on the long-term,

00:03:35.189 --> 00:03:37.749
what we're doing for the long-term,

00:03:37.749 --> 00:03:41.691
and I think that many of
the things we started up

00:03:41.691 --> 00:03:43.665
that are really big now, like Chrome,

00:03:43.665 --> 00:03:46.800
were seen as kind of crazy
when we launched them.

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:49.480
And, so how do we decide what to do?

00:03:49.480 --> 00:03:52.534
How do we decide what's
really important to work on?

00:03:52.534 --> 00:03:55.791
Well, I like to call it
the "Toothbrush Test."

00:03:55.791 --> 00:03:58.201
So, the Toothbrush Test is, do you use it

00:03:58.201 --> 00:04:01.231
as often as you use your toothbrush?

00:04:01.231 --> 00:04:03.083
For most people, I guess,
that's twice a day.

00:04:03.083 --> 00:04:04.805
Raise your hands, twice a day?

00:04:04.805 --> 00:04:06.805
Yeah, okay, most people.

00:04:09.196 --> 00:04:11.291
So I think, you know, we
really want things like that

00:04:11.291 --> 00:04:13.977
and I think things like
Gmail, obviously you use

00:04:13.977 --> 00:04:18.524
much more than twice a day,
and YouTube, you know...

00:04:18.524 --> 00:04:22.627
I think that those things are
amazing, and I think that...

00:04:22.627 --> 00:04:26.262
We look at things like
YouTube, people thought,

00:04:26.262 --> 00:04:28.724
"Oh, you guys are never going
to make money with that.

00:04:28.724 --> 00:04:32.455
You bought it for $1.4
billion, you're totally crazy."

00:04:32.455 --> 00:04:37.182
We were reasonably crazy,
but it was a good bet.

00:04:37.182 --> 00:04:39.654
And we've actually been doubling revenue

00:04:39.654 --> 00:04:42.185
every year on YouTube for four years.

00:04:42.185 --> 00:04:44.519
Actually, if you're doubling things,

00:04:44.519 --> 00:04:46.510
it starts to add up pretty quickly.

00:04:46.510 --> 00:04:48.400
(audience members chuckle)

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:51.067
Even, no matter where you start from.

00:04:51.067 --> 00:04:53.892
So, I think that's a
good example of the...

00:04:53.892 --> 00:04:56.227
How it's sort of our
philosophy, is that...

00:04:56.227 --> 00:04:59.032
we see things that
people use a lot that are

00:04:59.032 --> 00:05:01.567
going to be really important to them,

00:05:01.567 --> 00:05:03.666
and we think that usually,
you can make money

00:05:03.666 --> 00:05:06.098
from those things over time, you know...

00:05:06.098 --> 00:05:10.916
A well-run technology business
can be monetized over time.

00:05:10.916 --> 00:05:13.099
I think a lot of companies,
not so much these days,

00:05:13.099 --> 00:05:15.978
but still got started
because some space is hot.

00:05:15.978 --> 00:05:17.903
And honestly, I don't think that's

00:05:17.903 --> 00:05:21.070
a very good reason to start a company.

00:05:22.008 --> 00:05:26.229
And, in fact, we see hundreds
or thousands of people

00:05:26.229 --> 00:05:28.504
come to us who want to
commercialize something,

00:05:28.504 --> 00:05:31.001
or want us to collaborate
with their business,

00:05:31.001 --> 00:05:32.726
and we actually look at these...

00:05:32.726 --> 00:05:35.023
I look at quite a few
of them, and my guess is

00:05:35.023 --> 00:05:38.002
like, one a year we are interested in.

00:05:38.002 --> 00:05:41.728
So, you know, the trick
is to have the one that

00:05:41.728 --> 00:05:44.341
somebody's going to be interested
in 'cause it's a good idea,

00:05:44.341 --> 00:05:46.387
and not to, you know,
be one of the thousands

00:05:46.387 --> 00:05:48.018
that come through all the time.

00:05:48.018 --> 00:05:50.131
And I think, you know, if
you're the one good deal,

00:05:50.131 --> 00:05:52.040
or if you have a good idea, or you really

00:05:52.040 --> 00:05:53.811
understand the area you're in, you know,

00:05:53.811 --> 00:05:55.441
the funding environment doesn't matter.

00:05:55.441 --> 00:05:57.022
It doesn't matter whether it's a hot area.

00:05:57.022 --> 00:05:58.557
I guarantee you you'll be able to get

00:05:58.557 --> 00:06:00.290
people interested in helping you out.

00:06:00.290 --> 00:06:03.767
I think for all of you,
there are many areas

00:06:03.767 --> 00:06:05.768
where there's leverage
available in the world...

00:06:05.768 --> 00:06:08.753
Where one or two people, a good idea,

00:06:08.753 --> 00:06:11.516
a lot of hard work can
really make a difference.

00:06:11.516 --> 00:06:13.309
There's many, many things like that.

00:06:13.309 --> 00:06:15.167
And it's good not forget that,

00:06:15.167 --> 00:06:18.417
but it's good to look for those things.

00:06:19.304 --> 00:06:22.263
Google's mission is to organize
the world's information

00:06:22.263 --> 00:06:25.161
and make it universally
accessible and useful,

00:06:25.161 --> 00:06:27.616
and one of the things that surprised me...

00:06:27.616 --> 00:06:29.213
That's a very broad mission.

00:06:29.213 --> 00:06:30.183
It's a lot of work.

00:06:30.183 --> 00:06:33.219
We're not going to achieve
it anytime soon, right?

00:06:33.219 --> 00:06:35.507
It's almost an impossible task,

00:06:35.507 --> 00:06:38.121
but it's an exciting task
and one that you can get

00:06:38.121 --> 00:06:42.152
other people excited about,
and they can help you.

00:06:42.152 --> 00:06:43.853
And of the things that
also surprised me is

00:06:43.853 --> 00:06:46.860
it's sometimes easier to
do something that's harder

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:48.971
because other people get excited about it,

00:06:48.971 --> 00:06:51.408
and you can get much more resource.

00:06:51.408 --> 00:06:52.759
You can get tremendous resources

00:06:52.759 --> 00:06:55.068
to solve a hard problem,
whereas you might only

00:06:55.068 --> 00:06:57.703
get minor resources to
solve a small problem.

00:06:57.703 --> 00:06:59.197
You know, anything you can imagine

00:06:59.197 --> 00:07:00.741
probably is doable, right?

00:07:00.741 --> 00:07:05.028
You just have to imagine
it and work on it.

00:07:05.028 --> 00:07:07.926
And things that we thought
were almost impossible...

00:07:07.926 --> 00:07:10.869
So, we were interested
in machine translation.

00:07:10.869 --> 00:07:13.710
There were services on the
web, we had licensed one

00:07:13.710 --> 00:07:16.854
that could translate
between different languages.

00:07:16.854 --> 00:07:19.667
And, obviously, for Search,
you know, if you can't...

00:07:19.667 --> 00:07:21.704
If the information's not in your language,

00:07:21.704 --> 00:07:23.930
we can't find it for you,

00:07:23.930 --> 00:07:27.272
and most information's not
in your language, right?

00:07:27.272 --> 00:07:29.793
That's just sort of by definition.

00:07:29.793 --> 00:07:31.628
And, so we thought, "Well,
it'd be great if we could

00:07:31.628 --> 00:07:35.290
provide search results from
any language for any query."

00:07:35.290 --> 00:07:38.129
And so we really focused on that problem.

00:07:38.129 --> 00:07:41.902
We focused on it and we
developed Google Translate.

00:07:41.902 --> 00:07:44.439
We found some machine-learning
researchers, and we said,

00:07:44.439 --> 00:07:46.192
"Do you think you can translate, you know,

00:07:46.192 --> 00:07:48.433
between any languages, and do it better

00:07:48.433 --> 00:07:51.573
than an average human
translator," and they said...

00:07:51.573 --> 00:07:52.706
They laughed at us.

00:07:52.706 --> 00:07:54.363
They said, "No, we
can't possibly do that,"

00:07:54.363 --> 00:07:57.530
but they actually were willing to try.

00:07:58.418 --> 00:08:03.111
Six years later, we can
translate between 64 languages...

00:08:03.111 --> 00:08:04.574
(clears throat)

00:08:04.574 --> 00:08:07.648
And it's amazing,
actually, we're better...

00:08:07.648 --> 00:08:09.024
Many languages were better than

00:08:09.024 --> 00:08:11.474
an average human translating.

00:08:11.474 --> 00:08:13.897
We can do it instantly and for free.

00:08:13.897 --> 00:08:16.156
And between those 64
languages, we can translate

00:08:16.156 --> 00:08:18.604
between any two of the
languages, so we have something

00:08:18.604 --> 00:08:22.935
like 4,000 languages we
can translate between.

00:08:22.935 --> 00:08:25.575
So I'm just trying to give you...

00:08:25.575 --> 00:08:28.300
I guess I'm tremendously
optimistic that whatever things

00:08:28.300 --> 00:08:32.508
we try to challenge, whatever
challenges we try to take on,

00:08:32.508 --> 00:08:36.003
we can solve with a little
bit of concerted effort

00:08:36.003 --> 00:08:38.656
and some good technology,
and I think that's

00:08:38.656 --> 00:08:42.174
a really exciting place
to be in the world.

00:08:42.174 --> 00:08:43.962
So I think we want more
people working on it,

00:08:43.962 --> 00:08:46.853
we want to have more ambitious goals,

00:08:46.853 --> 00:08:50.444
and I think with that we could
easily double human progress

00:08:50.444 --> 00:08:53.331
and the rate at which we're developing.

00:08:53.331 --> 00:08:54.643
So, I'm going to switch gears and talk

00:08:54.643 --> 00:08:56.516
really briefly about entrepreneurship.

00:08:56.516 --> 00:08:58.382
So, I'll try to give
you some of the things

00:08:58.382 --> 00:09:00.324
I think are really
important that other people

00:09:00.324 --> 00:09:02.787
won't tell you, or won't stress as much.

00:09:02.787 --> 00:09:04.912
One of those is really just, don't settle.

00:09:04.912 --> 00:09:07.840
It's very, very, very important
if you're starting a company

00:09:07.840 --> 00:09:09.669
to have the right people involved.

00:09:09.669 --> 00:09:11.294
I can't stress that enough.

00:09:11.294 --> 00:09:14.152
I've been enormously happy
with the people we've had

00:09:14.152 --> 00:09:17.381
involved at Google, with my
co-founder and with Eric.

00:09:17.381 --> 00:09:20.410
We took a long time to find
these people, actually.

00:09:20.410 --> 00:09:22.399
I'd been working with Sergey
for a really long time,

00:09:22.399 --> 00:09:25.604
but Eric, took us over a
year to hire Eric, and...

00:09:25.604 --> 00:09:28.612
Just generally, having
great people involved

00:09:28.612 --> 00:09:31.194
that you really like and
that are compatible with you

00:09:31.194 --> 00:09:33.249
and all those things, is
tremendously important.

00:09:33.249 --> 00:09:35.810
You're never going to question
the equity you gave up,

00:09:35.810 --> 00:09:37.433
or any of the other things.

00:09:37.433 --> 00:09:38.968
So I can't stress that enough.

00:09:38.968 --> 00:09:40.466
The other thing I think we really

00:09:40.466 --> 00:09:43.037
benefited from is being real experts.

00:09:43.037 --> 00:09:46.931
We worked on Google for
many years at Stanford

00:09:46.931 --> 00:09:49.223
before we started the company,

00:09:49.223 --> 00:09:51.191
and that was a pretty
nice position to be in.

00:09:51.191 --> 00:09:53.224
We understood sort of
all aspects of Search,

00:09:53.224 --> 00:09:56.560
we talked to all the Search
companies for many years.

00:09:56.560 --> 00:09:59.313
We really knew a lot
about what was going on,

00:09:59.313 --> 00:10:00.840
and you can do that pretty cheaply, right?

00:10:00.840 --> 00:10:02.361
It's just your labor, right?

00:10:02.361 --> 00:10:04.555
You can invest a year
or two, or three years,

00:10:04.555 --> 00:10:07.655
and really learn something
very, very well before you start

00:10:07.655 --> 00:10:10.968
having hundreds of people
working on the problem.

00:10:10.968 --> 00:10:15.917
Recently, I reorganized a bit
to focus on product areas,

00:10:15.917 --> 00:10:18.584
and I think, you know, in
any company, as it grows,

00:10:18.584 --> 00:10:21.742
if the company's not static,
which it shouldn't be,

00:10:21.742 --> 00:10:24.711
as it grows and it changes you need to

00:10:24.711 --> 00:10:26.978
reorganize it and just question,

00:10:26.978 --> 00:10:30.356
you know, are you keeping ahead of that?

00:10:30.356 --> 00:10:33.854
Looking at our business
was pretty complicated.

00:10:33.854 --> 00:10:36.585
We have all these different
products, we have advertising,

00:10:36.585 --> 00:10:39.070
we have Search, we have
Chrome, we have Andriod,

00:10:39.070 --> 00:10:43.000
we have YouTube and so on,
and those things are actually

00:10:43.000 --> 00:10:46.756
relatively different and have
different things going on.

00:10:46.756 --> 00:10:49.824
So I think, you know, I kind
of moved that up a level

00:10:49.824 --> 00:10:51.435
in the company, and made sure that we were

00:10:51.435 --> 00:10:53.717
super focused in each of those areas,

00:10:53.717 --> 00:10:57.954
on what our user experience
was, and I think that's helped.

00:10:57.954 --> 00:10:59.870
Which, just generally, as
you switch to a company...

00:10:59.870 --> 00:11:01.620
As we got more offices, you know,

00:11:01.620 --> 00:11:03.911
as we got time zone differences,

00:11:03.911 --> 00:11:06.041
we had to change how we did our meetings,

00:11:06.041 --> 00:11:08.734
change how we do our employee
communications and so on...

00:11:08.734 --> 00:11:10.088
So, I just think, you know...

00:11:10.088 --> 00:11:12.288
In a technology business growing rapidly,

00:11:12.288 --> 00:11:14.946
you just need to make sure
you're changing everything.

00:11:14.946 --> 00:11:17.261
You're being responsive to that change

00:11:17.261 --> 00:11:19.387
and changing everything every year,

00:11:19.387 --> 00:11:21.193
or else you're just
not keeping up with it.

00:11:21.193 --> 00:11:24.014
I want to talk about dreams for a second.

00:11:24.014 --> 00:11:26.847
And in my case, literally a dream.

00:11:28.246 --> 00:11:31.001
When I was in college, I was sure

00:11:31.001 --> 00:11:33.982
that I'd been admitted
by a clerical error.

00:11:33.982 --> 00:11:35.096
(audience laughs)

00:11:35.096 --> 00:11:37.280
Probably a computer error. (laughter)

00:11:37.280 --> 00:11:40.345
And because of that, I
had an irrational fear

00:11:40.345 --> 00:11:43.265
I'd be sent home on the bus.

00:11:43.265 --> 00:11:45.309
And Sergey is here and knows this is true.

00:11:45.309 --> 00:11:47.532
(audience laughs)

00:11:47.532 --> 00:11:50.527
But it turns out because of that anxiety,

00:11:50.527 --> 00:11:53.697
I woke up literally with a dream,

00:11:53.697 --> 00:11:56.216
and it was kind of a strange dream.

00:11:56.216 --> 00:12:00.128
It went like, "I think I
could download the entire web

00:12:00.128 --> 00:12:03.559
onto some old computers
that were lying around."

00:12:03.559 --> 00:12:04.718
(audience laughs)

00:12:04.718 --> 00:12:08.555
And that probably seemed
pretty crazy to most people,

00:12:08.555 --> 00:12:11.617
but I stayed up a couple hours
in the middle of the night

00:12:11.617 --> 00:12:15.784
doing some math, and it seemed
actually pretty plausible.

00:12:17.025 --> 00:12:18.769
While assuming you
actually didn't keep any of

00:12:18.769 --> 00:12:22.019
the web pages, you only kept the links.

00:12:23.279 --> 00:12:26.546
And then, sort of figured
out, given all that data,

00:12:26.546 --> 00:12:28.879
I thought it would take a couple weeks,

00:12:28.879 --> 00:12:31.129
and I told my advisor that,

00:12:32.265 --> 00:12:34.727
and he just sort of laughed to me.

00:12:34.727 --> 00:12:37.589
And, of course, it took a year or two.

00:12:37.589 --> 00:12:42.204
But at the end of that, we
had a way to rank web pages,

00:12:42.204 --> 00:12:44.634
and no thoughts to Search at all,

00:12:44.634 --> 00:12:47.875
but eventually Search entered the picture.

00:12:47.875 --> 00:12:51.293
And you know the rest,
and that became Google.

00:12:51.293 --> 00:12:52.969
(applause)

00:12:52.969 --> 00:12:57.325
So I'd like to encourage
everyone to follow their dreams.

00:12:57.325 --> 00:12:59.172
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:12:59.172 --> 00:13:02.566
I made this video because
SickNick95 asked me to,

00:13:02.566 --> 00:13:04.600
so if there's a famous
entrepreneur that you want me to

00:13:04.600 --> 00:13:06.648
profile next, leave it
in the comments below,

00:13:06.648 --> 00:13:08.175
and I'll see what I can do.

00:13:08.175 --> 00:13:10.028
I'd also love to know
which of Larry Page's

00:13:10.028 --> 00:13:12.465
Top 10 Rules meant the most to you,

00:13:12.465 --> 00:13:14.422
is going to have the
biggest impact on you.

00:13:14.422 --> 00:13:15.598
Leave it in the comments below

00:13:15.598 --> 00:13:17.375
and I'm going to join in the discussion.

00:13:17.375 --> 00:13:18.924
Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:13:18.924 --> 00:13:22.507
Continue to believe,
and I'll see you soon.

